{"body":"## Summary\n\nAnnotated tags do not actually work as release keys in `radicle-artifact`\non `main`, despite appearing to be supported by the CLI. The root cause\nis a constraint in the underlying radicle COB store. This document\ncaptures the bug on `main`, the fix that was attempted on the\n[`link-annotated-tags` branch](https://radicle.network/nodes/daniel.radicle.garden/rad:z4VYyJ9KuwMNkXGQnmKuGPGKw3inv/remotes/z6MktwkohCx8aHZ1QCjVZUiLmX92oPZFxRiFZkbq32Tk5Tkm/tree/link-annotated-tags), why that approach hit a wall, and the\ndesign options for a real fix.\n\n## Background\n\nEvery release is a COB parented at a single git OID, and that OID is\nsurfaced on the CLI as `<commit>`. Two code paths resolve a\nuser-provided ref to an OID:\n\n- `resolve_commit(rev)` — used by every non-interactive command\n  (`add --commit`, `show`, `attest`, `redact`, `location add/remove`,\n  `fetch`). Internally calls `git revparse_single`, which for an\n  annotated tag returns the **tag object's OID**, not the commit it\n  points at.\n- `pick_commit_or_tag` — the interactive picker in `rad-artifact add`.\n  Lists annotated tags alongside recent commits, but peels each tag to\n  its commit before returning, so the OID passed on is always a\n  commit.\n\n## The bug on `main`\n\nThe COB store requires the OID used as a COB's parent to be a\n**commit object**. As a result, annotated tags are broken end-to-end:\n\n- `rad-artifact add --commit v1.0 --name foo ./build` fails at COB\n  creation time. `revparse_single(\"v1.0\")` returns the tag object OID;\n  `find_or_create_by_oid` then tries to parent the COB at that OID and\n  the store rejects it:\n\n  ```\n  ERROR: failed to create release for commit aa077f1...\n  caused by: the parent 'aa077f1...' provided is not a commit object\n  caused by: Object 'aa077f1...' is not expected object type commit;\n  class=Object (11); code=NotFound (-3)\n  ```\n\n- Every read path that accepts a `<commit>` arg — `show`, `attest`,\n  `redact`, `location add/remove`, `fetch` — routes through the same\n  `resolve_commit` and then calls `find_unique_by_oid(tag_oid)`. Since\n  no COB can ever successfully be created under a tag OID, those\n  lookups always miss.\n\n- The interactive picker *appears* to work against tags: the user\n  picks `v1.0`, a release is created, and no error is shown. But the\n  COB is silently keyed by the peeled commit OID, and no record of\n  the tag (name or OID) is preserved. The release is commit-keyed;\n  `show v1.0` from the same terminal immediately afterwards fails to\n  find it because the read path doesn't peel. Tags are a facade.\n\nSo on `main` the effective behavior is: releases are always\ncommit-keyed, tags are not remembered, and three of the four entry\npoints that take a tag name fail outright.\n\n## What the branch tried\n\nBranch `link-annotated-tags`. Two commits:\n\n- `8de8a6b` — picker fix. Store the tag object OID in the picker's\n  `Entry::Tag` instead of the peeled commit OID. Don't drop the peeled\n  commit from the dedup set, so both the tag and the underlying\n  commit remain selectable.\n- `a104cdb` — display scaffolding. Add `display::RefKind`\n  (`Commit` | `Tag`) and a `ref_kind` method on `CommitTitle`.\n  `CommitTitle for Repository` learned to return the tag message's\n  first line for tag OIDs (falling back to the peeled commit summary\n  for tags with no message). Pretty output prints\n  `tag <short>` / `commit <short>` based on the resolved object\n  type; JSON gains a `ref_kind` field.\n\nThe intent was to make the picker's tag entry match the OID that\n`resolve_commit` already returns for a tag name, then have the COB\ncarry the tag identity.\n\n## Why that hit a wall\n\nThe COB parent must be a commit. Storing the tag OID in\n`Entry::Tag` made the picker pass a tag OID into\n`find_or_create_by_oid`, and the store rejected it with the same\n\"not a commit object\" error described above. The branch reproduced\nthe exact failure that was already latent on `main` — it just moved\nit from the non-interactive path into the picker path as well.\n\nThe constraint lives inside the `radicle` crate's COB machinery and\ncan't be relaxed from this repo.\n\n## Options for a real fix\n\n### Option 1 — COB stays commit-keyed, tag recorded as release metadata\n\nAlways peel tag → commit before creating or looking up the COB. Add\nan optional field to the release content (inside the COB payload)\nthat records the associated annotated tag: name, OID, or both.\n\n- `rad-artifact add` peels before creating the release, and stores\n  the tag reference in the release content.\n- `show v1.0` and `show <commit>` both peel to the same commit OID\n  and find the same COB.\n- Display renders `tag v1.0` when the release content carries a tag\n  reference; otherwise `commit <short>`.\n\nDownsides:\n- Two distinct tags pointing at the same commit have to be\n  disambiguated: first writer wins, append and show all, or pick\n  one at display time.\n- The COB payload grows a new optional field. Existing COBs\n  trivially lack it and render as commit-keyed, so no active\n  migration is needed.\n\n### Option 2 — Separate COB type for tag-keyed releases\n\nIntroduce a second COB type keyed by the tag object OID, using a\nparent mechanism that doesn't require a commit. This means changes\nto the `radicle` COB store itself and is out of scope for this\nrepo.\n\n### Option 3 — Drop the feature, make the failure consistent\n\nAccept that releases are commit-keyed only. Fix the bug on `main`\nby having `resolve_commit` peel annotated tag refs to commits, so\nall entry points agree and `--commit v1.0` just works as a\nhuman-friendly alias for the commit. Smallest change; sacrifices\nthe \"link to tag\" goal entirely.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nOption 1. It satisfies the COB store's constraint, keeps a single\ncanonical OID per release, and surfaces the tag exactly where the\nuser sees it.\n\nStaging:\n\n1. Fix the `main` bug: have `resolve_commit` peel annotated tag refs\n   to commits, so every entry point resolves `v1.0` to the commit\n   the COB is actually keyed by. This unbreaks\n   `add --commit v1.0`, `show v1.0`, `attest v1.0`, etc. on `main`\n   independently of any schema change.\n2. Extend the release content schema with an optional `tag` field\n   (name + OID, or just one of them).\n3. Update `rad-artifact add` to record the tag reference when the\n   user adds a release via a tag.\n4. Drive `RefKind` and the pretty-output label from the release\n   content's `tag` field instead of the stored OID's object type\n   (the scaffolding from `a104cdb` can be kept or re-derived as\n   needed).\n\n## Immediate next step for the branch\n\nThe picker change in `8de8a6b` surfaces the COB's\n\"not a commit object\" error and must not ship as-is. Either:\n\n- revert `8de8a6b` and keep `a104cdb` as standalone display\n  scaffolding (but that scaffolding currently reads object type\n  from the stored OID — with the picker reverted, stored OIDs will\n  always be commits, so `RefKind::Tag` will never be produced until\n  step 3 above is done), or\n- revert both commits and reintroduce them alongside the Option 1\n  schema change.\n","type":"comment"}